  #1.Create a set to keep only unique groups of four numbers.
#2.Run the first loop from the start to the end of the array to pick the first number.
#3.Inside it, run the second loop from the next position to pick the second number.
#4.Before starting the third loop, make a HashSet to keep track of numbers between the second and third positions.
#5.Run the third loop from the next position after the second number to the end of the array to pick the third number.
#6.Find the fourth number by subtracting the total of the first three numbers from the target value.
#7.If this fourth number is already in the HashSet, arrange all four numbers in order and add them to the set.
#8.Add the current third number to the HashSet (only numbers between the second and third loops are stored).
#9.After all loops finish, return the set as a list of unique groups of four numbers.
#time complexity O(N3*log(M))
#space complexity O(no. of quadruplets)
class Solution:
    def fourSum(self, arr, target):
        n = len(arr)
        st = set()  

        for i in range(n):
            for j in range(i + 1, n):
                seen = set()  

                for k in range(j + 1, n):
                    required = target - arr[i] - arr[j] - arr[k]

                    if required in seen:
                        temp = tuple(sorted([arr[i], arr[j], arr[k], required]))
                        st.add(temp)

                    seen.add(arr[k])

        return [list(quad) for quad in st]
